Much material in these R-based lectures come from material provided by Arthur Li, City of Hope National Medical Center (used with permission), and some parts may have been derived from various online sources, such as “Advanced R”, by Hadley Wickham.

R language - Definition and Background

	•	Definition from Wikipedia® 
	•	R is a programming language and software environment for statistical computing and graphics.
	•	The R language has become a de facto standard among statisticians for the development of statistical software.
	•	R is widely used for statistical software development and data analysis.

	•	History of R

	•	R is an implementation of the S programming language
	•	S was created by John Chambers
	•	Another commercial implementation of S language from Insightful Corporation is called S-SPLUS®
	•	R is named partly after the first names of the first two R authors (Robert Gentleman and Ross Ihaka)
	•	R is part of the GNU project. Its source code is freely available under the GNU General Public License

R System

	•	The R System consists of two conceptual parts: 
	•	The “base” R sytem that you download from CRAN - This is what you want to install R for the first time Contributed packages.
	•	The “base” R system contains the base package which is required to run R and contains the most fundamental functions. 
	•	The main installation will install R and a popular set of add-on libraries
	•	You can install hundreds of libraries from the Comprehensive R Archive Network (CRAN) or the Bioconductor 
	•	Package installations 
	•	To install a new package from the CRAN, you can use the install.packages function. For example, to install the “Epi” package, type 
> install.packages("Epi") 
	•	Alternatively, you can also click on the “Packages” tab from the toolbar in the R window, then click on “Install package(s),” then follow the remainder of the instructions 
	•	Once a package is installed, it is stored on the computer’s hard drive 
> library (Epi)

R front-ends

	•	R comes with basic facilities to edit and R programs. 
	•	The structure of these default front-ends varies by operating system (e.g. Windows, MacOS, Linux) 
	•	Many Alternative front-ends are available in the form of editors (e.g. Emacs) and Integrated Development Environments (IDE’s) (e.g. Eclipse, Rstudio). 
	•	We will make use of the free open-source version of the Rstudio IDE (https://www.rstudio.com).  
	•	Note that Rstudio is a front-end to R and is not the same thing as R!

Use R as a Calculator

	•	Of the many functions R can accomplish, it can even serve as a simple calculator. For example: 
> 3*2+4
[1] 10
> 4^2+1
[1] 17
> log(2)+exp(5)
[1] 149.1063 
	•	You type the command after the prompt >
	•	The second line is the output

Expressions and Assignments

	•	Commands in R are either expressions or assignments
	•	Commands are separated by either a semi-colon or a new line
	•	An expression command is evaluated and (normally) printed 
> 5+2;

1+exp(2)

[1] 7
[1] 8.389056 
	•	You can assign a value to a variable by using <- or =, but not < . When typing the name of an object, R will print this object or a short summary of the object. This is refers as auto-printing.
	•	You can also use the print function explicitly like the one below.
	•	An assignment command evaluates an expression and passes the value onto a variable, but the result is not printed. 
> a = 5*pi
> a
[1] 15.70796
> print(a)
[1] 15.70796

Naming Conventions

	•	R names for objects can be made up from: 
◦ upper and/or lower case letters
◦ digits: 0 to 9, in any non-initial position
◦ periods and underscores, usually used to separate words in names 
	•	R names are case sensitive 
	•	Avoid using system names such as 
◦ c, q, s, t, C, D, F, I, T, diff, length, mean, pi, range, rank, time, tree, and var
◦ Using these names will probably prompt a warning message
◦ Sometimes, using these names will cause a computation error 
	•	You are not allowed to use the following reserved words: FALSE, Inf, NA, NaN, NULL, TRUE, break, else, for, function, if, in, next, repeat, return and while

Vectorized Arithmetic

	•	A data vector is an array of numbers that can be constructed by using c(...)
	•	To construct vectors storing the weights and heights for a group of people, you can type the following command: 
> weight = c(60, 72, 57, 90, 95, 72)
> weight 
[1] 60 72 57 90 95 72 
> height = c(1.75, 1.80, 1.65, 1.90, 1.74, 1.91) 
	•	You can perform the calculations with vectors of the same length. For example, 
> bmi = weight/height^2
> bmi 
[1] 19.59184 22.22222 20.93664 24.93075 31.37799 19.73630 
	•	It is also possible to perform the calculations with vectors of different lengths
	•	You have already used calculations with vectors of different lengths in height2 since 2 is a vector with a length 1
	•	The shorter vector is recycled
	•	If the longer vector is not a multiple of the shorter vector, R will issue a warning message:
	•	longer object length is not a multiple of shorter object length
	•	To calculate the mean of the bmi, you can type the following 
> sum(height)
[1] 10.75
> length(height)
[1] 6
> sum(height)/length(height)
[1] 1.791667 
	•	Or you can simply type 
> mean(height)
[1] 1.791667

