Miscellaneous Matters

	•	Everything typed after # be ignored by R 
> foo = 10 # foo is assigned with value 10 
	•	The previous command can be recalled with the up-arrow key

The “help” Function

	•	You can use the help (?) function to read the description of the function. For example, help(var) or ?var 
	•	For operators, such as +, -, [, [[, you need to enclosed it in “”. For example,

> help(“+”)



	•	To find out the arguments of a function, we can use the args function. For example, 
> args(var)
 function (x, y = NULL, na.rm = FALSE, use)
NULL 
	•	If we do not know the name of the function, we can use the help.search function. For example 
> help.search("linear models")

An Overview of R Objects

Mode and Class

	•	Everything within the R language is an object 
	•	The nature of R objects can be described by their own attributes. The number of attributes for each object vary 
	•	When managing objects in R , it is critical and important to distinguish different R objects and to understand the difference between each object 
	•	The two most important attributes of R objects are mode and class, which describe the characteristics of the objects 
	•	You can find the mode of each object by using the mode function, which describes the data type or storage mode of an object. For example, 
> n1 = c(1, 4, pi, 10)
> n1
[1]

1.000000

4.000000

3.141593 10.000000

> mode(n1)
[1] "numeric"

	•	The most commonly encountered modes are numeric, character, and logical 
	•	The class attribute describes the data structure of an object 
	•	The concept of class is closely related to objected-oriented programming 
	•	You can find the class of each object by using the the class function. For example, the following command creates a matrix, n2 from n1, by using the matrix function

> n2 = matrix(n1, nrow=2)
> n2

[1,]
[2,]

[,1]
[,2]
1 3.141593
4 10.000000

> mode(n2)
[1] "numeric"
> class(n2)
[1] "matrix"
> class(n1)
[1] "numeric"

	•	Notice that the mode of n2 is still numeric. The difference between n1 and n2 is distinguished by their class. n1 is a one-dimensional vector, whereas n2 is a matrix 
	•	Even n1 is a vector, but the class function does not return vector ; instead, it returns “numeric.”


Missing Values

	•	NA is used for missing elements in numeric, character, and logical vectors 
	•	NA is not a value but a marker for a missing value 
	•	The missing values are sometimes intended or may result from computation or data type conversion, for example, 
> c1 = c("A", "B", "C")
> as.numeric(c1)
[1] NA NA NA
Warning message:
In eval(expr, envir, enclos) : NAs introduced by coercion

	•	The is.na function can be used to indicate which elements of a vector are missing 
	•	The length of the result from the is.na function is the same as its argument. For example, 
> n4 = c(1, NA, 3)
> is.na(n4)
[1] FALSE

TRUE FALSE 
	•	In general, operations on missing value(s) NA result in a missing value(s) NA
	•	Some functions have a na.rm argument. Setting the na.rm argument to TRUE will remove the missing values during operations 
> sum(n4)
[1] NA
> sum(n4, na.rm=T)
[1] 4


